Local rework of the Seurat vignette "Analysis, visualization, and integration of
spatial datasets with Seurat" (sequencing-based platforms).

Upstream: <https://satijalab.org/seurat/articles/spatial_vignette>

::: {.callout-important}
## Status: transcribed from upstream, not yet executed

Every chunk below is transcribed from the upstream Seurat vignette and has **not
been run yet**. This first render is the test. `error: false` is set in the
YAML, so any chunk failure stops the render and is reported rather than being
printed into the document and passed over. Apart from the three interactive
Shiny chunks noted below, every chunk has `eval: true`, so nothing is skipped.
:::

::: {.callout-note}
## Data files this document expects

Upstream loads data through `SeuratData` (`InstallData()` / `LoadData()`) and
through absolute paths on the Satija Lab file server
(`/brahms/shared/vignette-data/...`). Both are network / server calls, so they
are replaced here by plain `readRDS()` from the workshop data directory.

The staging script must place these files in the data directory for
`spatial_visium`:

- `stxBrain_anterior1.rds` , the `stxBrain` `Seurat` object for the anterior1
  sagittal mouse brain slice, i.e. `saveRDS(SeuratData::LoadData("stxBrain",
  type = "anterior1"), ...)` run on a machine with network access.
- `stxBrain_posterior1.rds` , the same for `type = "posterior1"`.
- `allen_cortex.rds` , the ~14k cell Allen Institute adult mouse cortex
  SMART-Seq2 scRNA-seq reference (`Seurat` object). Upstream reads this from
  `/brahms/shared/vignette-data/allen_cortex.rds`.
- `mouse_hippocampus_reference.rds` , the processed mouse hippocampus scRNA-seq
  reference `Seurat` object (Saunders, Macosko et al. 2018). Upstream reads this
  from `/brahms/shared/vignette-data/mouse_hippocampus_reference.rds`.
- `ssHippo.rds` , the `ssHippo` `Seurat` object (Slide-seq v2 mouse
  hippocampus), i.e. `saveRDS(SeuratData::LoadData("ssHippo"), ...)`.
:::

::: {.callout-warning}
## Slow steps that could be precomputed at staging time

Several steps are slow. If the first test render takes too long, precompute them
on a staging machine and stage the resulting objects instead:

- `SCTransform()` on each brain slice and on the Allen reference.
- `FindSpatiallyVariableFeatures(selection.method = "moransi")`, run three times
  (brain genes, cortex prediction scores, Slide-seq genes). This is the slowest
  family of calls. Installing the optional `Rfast2` package speeds up the
  Slide-seq binning path.
- The anchor-based integration (`FindTransferAnchors()` / `TransferData()`),
  run for both the Visium cortex and the Slide-seq puck.
- RCTD deconvolution (`create.RCTD()` / `run.RCTD()`), which also needs the
  `spacexr` package installed from GitHub.
:::

## Setup

```{r}
#| label: setup
source(here::here("slides", "find_data_dir.R"))
data_dir <- find_data_dir("spatial_visium")
# allen_cortex.rds is shared with the imaging tutorial and staged once at the
# top level of the data dir, so read it from the root rather than data_dir.
data_root <- find_data_dir()
report_data_dir("spatial_visium")
```

```{r}
#| label: libraries
library(Seurat)
library(ggplot2)
library(patchwork)
library(dplyr)
```

# 10x Visium

## Dataset

We will be using a dataset of sagittal mouse brain slices generated using the
Visium v1 chemistry. There are two serial anterior sections and two (matched)
serial posterior sections.

Upstream downloads the data with `SeuratData::InstallData("stxBrain")` and then
`LoadData("stxBrain", type = "anterior1")`. Here we read the staged object
instead.

```{r}
#| label: load-brain
brain <- readRDS(file.path(data_dir, "stxBrain_anterior1.rds"))
brain
```

## Data preprocessing

The initial preprocessing steps we perform on the spot-by-gene expression data
are similar to a typical scRNA-seq experiment. We first normalize the data to
account for variance in sequencing depth across data points. The variance in
molecular counts per spot can be substantial for spatial datasets, particularly
if there are differences in cell density across the tissue.

```{r}
#| label: brain-qc-plots
plot1 <- VlnPlot(brain, features = "nCount_Spatial", pt.size = 0.1) + NoLegend()

plot2 <- SpatialFeaturePlot(brain, features = "nCount_Spatial") + theme(legend.position = "right")

wrap_plots(plot1, plot2)
```

We proceed with `sctransform` (Hafemeister and Satija, Genome Biology 2019,
<https://doi.org/10.1186/s13059-019-1874-1>) for normalization, which builds
regularized negative binomial models of gene expression to account for technical
artifacts while preserving biological variance.

```{r}
#| label: brain-sctransform
brain <- SCTransform(brain, assay = "Spatial", verbose = FALSE)
```

## Gene expression visualization

`SpatialFeaturePlot()` extends `FeaturePlot()`, and can overlay molecular data on
top of tissue histology. In this mouse brain data set, `Hpca` is a strong
hippocampus marker and `Ttr` is a marker of the choroid plexus.

```{r}
#| label: brain-spatial-features
SpatialFeaturePlot(brain, features = c("Hpca", "Ttr"))
```

The spatial plotting functions return `ggplot2` objects, so additional layers
can be added for customization.

```{r}
#| label: brain-custom-plot
custom_plot <- SpatialFeaturePlot(brain, features = c("Ttr")) +
                theme(legend.text = element_text(size = 0),
                      legend.title = element_text(size = 20),
                      legend.key.size = unit(1, "cm"))

custom_plot
```

Users can also adjust the size of the spots (and their transparency) to improve
visualization of the histology image. Below we try a different scale factor for
point size and set `alpha = c(0.1, 1)`, to downweight the transparency of points
with lower expression.

```{r}
#| label: brain-pt-size-alpha
p1 <- SpatialFeaturePlot(brain, features = "Ttr", pt.size.factor = 1)

p2 <- SpatialFeaturePlot(brain, features = "Ttr", alpha = c(0.1, 1))

p1 + p2
```

## Dimensionality reduction, clustering, and visualization

We proceed to run dimensionality reduction and clustering on the RNA expression
data, using the same workflow as for scRNA-seq analysis.

```{r}
#| label: brain-cluster
brain <- RunPCA(brain, assay = "SCT", verbose = FALSE)
brain <- FindNeighbors(brain, reduction = "pca", dims = 1:30)
brain <- FindClusters(brain, verbose = FALSE)
brain <- RunUMAP(brain, reduction = "pca", dims = 1:30)
```

We can visualize the clustering either in UMAP space (with `DimPlot()`) or
overlaid on the image with `SpatialDimPlot()`.

```{r}
#| label: brain-dimplot
p1 <- DimPlot(brain, reduction = "umap", label = TRUE)

p2 <- SpatialDimPlot(brain, label = TRUE, label.size = 3)

p1 + p2
```

The `cells.highlight` parameter can demarcate particular cells of interest on a
`SpatialDimPlot()`, useful for distinguishing the spatial localization of
individual clusters.

```{r}
#| label: brain-cells-highlight
SpatialDimPlot(brain,
               cells.highlight = CellsByIdentities(object = brain, idents = c(2, 1, 4, 3, 5, 8)),
               facet.highlight = TRUE,
               ncol = 3) & theme(plot.margin = unit(c(1, 1, 1, 1), "mm"))
```

## Interactive plotting

::: {.callout-note}
The interactive plots below (`interactive = TRUE`, and `LinkedDimPlot()`) open a
Shiny session, which blocks a non-interactive render forever. Confirmed on
2026-07-26: `quarto render` hung on `brain-interactive-dim` for over an hour
before being killed. All three chunks are therefore `eval: false`, and the code
is shown but not run.

To see them work, run the three chunks by hand in an interactive R session.
:::

Both `SpatialDimPlot()` and `SpatialFeaturePlot()` have an `interactive`
parameter that, when set to `TRUE`, opens an interactive Shiny environment.

```{r}
#| label: brain-interactive-dim
#| eval: false
SpatialDimPlot(brain, interactive = TRUE)
```

For `SpatialFeaturePlot()`, setting `interactive` to `TRUE` allows for adjusting
the transparency of the spots, the point size, and the Assay and feature being
plotted.

```{r}
#| label: brain-interactive-feature
#| eval: false
SpatialFeaturePlot(brain, features = "Ttr", interactive = TRUE)
```

`LinkedDimPlot()` links the UMAP representation to the tissue image
representation and allows for interactive selection.

```{r}
#| label: brain-linked-dim
#| eval: false
LinkedDimPlot(brain)
```

## Identification of Spatially Variable Features

The first approach is to perform differential expression based on pre-annotated
anatomical regions within the tissue, which may be determined either from
unsupervised clustering or prior knowledge.

```{r}
#| label: brain-de-markers
de_markers <- FindMarkers(brain, ident.1 = 5, ident.2 = 7)

SpatialFeaturePlot(object = brain, features = rownames(de_markers)[1:3], alpha = c(0.1, 1), ncol = 3)
```

An alternative approach, implemented in `FindSpatiallyVariableFeatures()`,
searches for features exhibiting spatial patterning in the absence of
pre-annotation. Here we specify `selection.method = "moransi"`, which uses
Moran's I as a spatial autocorrelation metric.

::: {.callout-warning}
This `FindSpatiallyVariableFeatures()` call is one of the slow steps. Consider
precomputing at staging time.
:::

```{r}
#| label: brain-svf-moransi
brain <- FindSpatiallyVariableFeatures(brain,
                                       features = VariableFeatures(brain)[1:1000],
                                       selection.method = "moransi")
```

Now we visualize the expression of the top 6 features identified by this
measure.

```{r}
#| label: brain-svf-plot
top.features <- head(SpatiallyVariableFeatures(brain, method = "moransi"), 6)

SpatialFeaturePlot(brain, features = top.features, ncol = 3, alpha = c(0.1, 1)) &
  theme(plot.margin = unit(c(1, 1, 1, 1), "mm"))
```

## Subset out anatomical regions

Here we approximately subset the frontal cortex. First we take a subset of
clusters, then further segment based on exact positions.

```{r}
#| label: brain-subset-clusters
brain_sub <- subset(brain, idents = c(1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 7))

SpatialDimPlot(brain_sub, label = T, crop = T, label.size = 3) + NoLegend() +
  SpatialDimPlot(brain_sub, label = T, crop = F, label.size = 3)
```

Now we can examine approximately which region to subset to by plotting with the
axes labeled.

```{r}
#| label: brain-subset-axes
theme_axis_labels <- theme(axis.text.x = element_text(size = 10),
                           axis.text.y = element_text(size = 10),
                           axis.title.x = element_text(size = 10),
                           axis.title.y = element_text(size = 10))

SpatialDimPlot(brain_sub, label = T, label.size = 3, crop = T) + theme_axis_labels
```

We can zoom in to a particular region with `Crop()`.

```{r}
#| label: brain-crop
brain_sub[["anterior1"]] <- Crop(brain_sub[["anterior1"]],
                              x = c(150, 500),
                              y = c(0, 425))

SpatialDimPlot(brain_sub, label = T) + theme_axis_labels
```

Finally we subset out the cells in the lower right quadrant. Because our
coordinates are in image space, we need to be careful when subsetting using a
line. The y-coordinates in Cartesian space are negative, since the direction of
our y-axis is downward.

```{r}
#| label: brain-subset-cortex
m <- 0.6; b <- -550

cortex_coords <- GetTissueCoordinates(brain_sub, scale = "lowres") %>%
                  subset(-y >= (m * x + b))

cortex <- subset(brain_sub,
                 cells = cortex_coords$cell)
SpatialDimPlot(cortex, label = T, label.size = 3, crop = F)
```

```{r}
#| label: brain-cortex-crop-plots
c1 <- SpatialDimPlot(cortex, images = "anterior1", crop = T, label = T)
c2 <- SpatialDimPlot(cortex, images = "anterior1", crop = F, label = T, pt.size.factor = 1, label.size = 3)

c1 + c2
```

## Integration with single-cell data

At ~50um, spots from the Visium assay will encompass the expression profiles of
multiple cells. We apply the anchor-based integration workflow to transfer
annotations from a reference scRNA-seq dataset of ~14,000 adult mouse cortical
cells (Allen Institute, SMART-Seq2 protocol) to the spatial query.

Upstream reads the reference from
`/brahms/shared/vignette-data/allen_cortex.rds`. Here we read the staged object.

::: {.callout-warning}
`SCTransform()` on the reference and the `FindTransferAnchors()` /
`TransferData()` integration are slow steps. Consider precomputing.
:::

```{r}
#| label: allen-reference
allen_reference <- readRDS(file.path(data_root, "allen_cortex.rds"))
# Note: setting ncells=3000 normalizes the full dataset, but learns noise models on 3k cells
# This speeds up SCTransform dramatically with no loss in performance
allen_reference <- SCTransform(allen_reference, ncells = 3000, verbose = FALSE) %>%
                      RunPCA(verbose = FALSE) %>%
                      RunUMAP(dims = 1:30)
```

```{r}
#| label: allen-dimplot
# The annotation is stored in the 'subclass' column of object metadata
DimPlot(allen_reference, group.by = "subclass", label = TRUE)
```

```{r}
#| label: brain-transfer-anchors
anchors <- FindTransferAnchors(reference = allen_reference,
                               query = cortex)

predictions.assay <- TransferData(anchorset = anchors,
                                  refdata = allen_reference$subclass,
                                  prediction.assay = TRUE,
                                  weight.reduction = cortex[["pca"]],
                                  dims = 1:30)

cortex[["predictions"]] <- predictions.assay
```

Now we get prediction scores for each spot for each class. Of particular
interest in the frontal cortex region are the laminar excitatory neurons.

```{r}
#| label: cortex-prediction-plot
DefaultAssay(cortex) <- "predictions"

SpatialFeaturePlot(cortex, features = c("L2/3 IT", "L4"), ncol = 2, crop = TRUE) &
  theme(plot.margin = unit(c(1, 1, 1, 1), "mm"))
```

Based on these prediction scores, we can also predict cell types whose location
is spatially restricted, using the prediction scores as the "marks" rather than
gene expression.

::: {.callout-warning}
Another slow `FindSpatiallyVariableFeatures()` call.
:::

```{r}
#| label: cortex-svf
cortex <- FindSpatiallyVariableFeatures(cortex,
                                        assay = "predictions",
                                        selection.method = "moransi",
                                        features = rownames(cortex),
                                        r.metric = 5,
                                        layer = "data")

top.clusters <- head(SpatiallyVariableFeatures(cortex, method = "moransi"), 4)

SpatialFeaturePlot(object = cortex, features = top.clusters, ncol = 2) &
  theme(plot.margin = unit(c(1, 1, 1, 1), "mm"))
```

Finally, we show that our integrative procedure can recover known spatial
localization patterns of both neuronal and non-neuronal subsets.

```{r}
#| label: cortex-features-of-interest
features_of_interest <- c("Astro", "L2/3 IT", "L4", "L5 PT", "L5 IT", "L6 CT", "L6 IT", "L6b", "Oligo")

SpatialFeaturePlot(cortex,
                   features = features_of_interest,
                   pt.size.factor = 1,
                   ncol = 2,
                   crop = TRUE,
                   alpha = c(0.1, 1)) & theme(plot.margin = unit(c(1, 1, 1, 1), "mm")) &
                theme(plot.margin = unit(c(1, 1, 1, 1), "mm"))
```

## Working with multiple slices

This dataset contains another slice corresponding to the other half of the
brain. Upstream loads it with `LoadData("stxBrain", type = "posterior1")`. Here
we read the staged object and perform the same initial normalization.

```{r}
#| label: load-brain2
brain2 <- readRDS(file.path(data_dir, "stxBrain_posterior1.rds"))

brain2 <- SCTransform(brain2, assay = "Spatial", verbose = FALSE)
```

To work with multiple slices in the same Seurat object, we use the `merge`
function.

```{r}
#| label: brain-merge
brain.merge <- merge(brain, brain2)
```

This enables joint dimensional reduction and clustering on the underlying RNA
expression data.

```{r}
#| label: brain-merge-cluster
DefaultAssay(brain.merge) <- "SCT"
VariableFeatures(brain.merge) <- c(VariableFeatures(brain), VariableFeatures(brain2))
brain.merge <- RunPCA(brain.merge, verbose = FALSE)
brain.merge <- FindNeighbors(brain.merge, dims = 1:30)
brain.merge <- FindClusters(brain.merge, verbose = FALSE)
brain.merge <- RunUMAP(brain.merge, dims = 1:30)
```

The data can be jointly visualized in a single UMAP plot. `SpatialDimPlot()` and
`SpatialFeaturePlot()` will by default plot all slices as columns and
groupings/features as rows.

```{r}
#| label: brain-merge-dimplot
DimPlot(brain.merge, reduction = "umap", group.by = c("ident", "orig.ident"))
```

```{r}
#| label: brain-merge-spatial-dim
SpatialDimPlot(brain.merge)
```

```{r}
#| label: brain-merge-spatial-feature
SpatialFeaturePlot(brain.merge, features = c("Hpca", "Plp1"))
```

