We would like to thank Nigel Delaney and Stephen Williams for their helpful
feedback and contributions to the code for Visium data loading and analysis.

# Slide-seq

## Dataset

Here we analyze a dataset generated using Slide-seq v2 of the mouse hippocampus.
Upstream loads it with `SeuratData::InstallData("ssHippo")` and
`LoadData('ssHippo')`. Here we read the staged object.

```{r}
#| label: load-slideseq
slide.seq <- readRDS(file.path(data_dir, "ssHippo.rds"))
slide.seq
```

## Data preprocessing

Many beads contain particularly low UMI counts, but we choose to keep all
detected beads for downstream analysis.

```{r}
#| label: slideseq-qc-plots
plot1 <- VlnPlot(slide.seq, features = 'nCount_Spatial', pt.size = 0, log = TRUE) + NoLegend()
slide.seq$log_nCount_Spatial <- log(slide.seq$nCount_Spatial)
plot2 <- SpatialFeaturePlot(slide.seq, features = 'log_nCount_Spatial') + theme(legend.position = "right")
wrap_plots(plot1, plot2)
```

We then normalize the data using `sctransform` and perform a standard scRNA-seq
dimensionality reduction and clustering workflow.

```{r}
#| label: slideseq-cluster
slide.seq <- SCTransform(slide.seq, assay = "Spatial", ncells = 3000, verbose = FALSE)
slide.seq <- RunPCA(slide.seq)
slide.seq <- RunUMAP(slide.seq, dims = 1:30)
slide.seq <- FindNeighbors(slide.seq, dims = 1:30)
slide.seq <- FindClusters(slide.seq, resolution = 0.3, verbose = FALSE)
```

We can visualize the results of the clustering either in UMAP space (with
`DimPlot()`) or in the bead coordinate space with `SpatialDimPlot()`.

```{r}
#| label: slideseq-dimplot
plot1 <- DimPlot(slide.seq, reduction = "umap", label = TRUE)
plot2 <- SpatialDimPlot(slide.seq, stroke = 0)
plot1 + plot2
```

```{r}
#| label: slideseq-cells-highlight
SpatialDimPlot(slide.seq, cells.highlight = CellsByIdentities(object = slide.seq, idents = c(1, 6, 13)), facet.highlight = TRUE)
```

## Integration with a scRNA-seq reference

To facilitate cell-type annotation of the Slide-seq dataset, we leverage an
existing mouse single-cell RNA-seq hippocampus dataset, produced in Saunders,
Macosko, et al. 2018, <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2018.07.028>. Upstream
reads the processed Seurat object from
`/brahms/shared/vignette-data/mouse_hippocampus_reference.rds`. Here we read the
staged object.

::: {.callout-warning}
The `FindTransferAnchors()` / `TransferData()` integration is a slow step.
:::

```{r}
#| label: load-hippo-reference
ref <- readRDS(file.path(data_dir, "mouse_hippocampus_reference.rds"))
ref <- UpdateSeuratObject(ref)
```

The original annotations are provided at several resolutions, from broad classes
(`ref$class`) to subclusters within celltypes (`ref$subcluster`). For this
vignette we work off of a modification of the celltype annotations
(`ref$celltype`).

```{r}
#| label: slideseq-transfer-anchors
anchors <- FindTransferAnchors(reference = ref,
                               query = slide.seq,
                               normalization.method = "SCT",
                               npcs = 50)

predictions.assay <- TransferData(anchorset = anchors,
                                  refdata = ref$celltype,
                                  prediction.assay = TRUE,
                                  weight.reduction = slide.seq[["pca"]],
                                  dims = 1:50)

slide.seq[["predictions"]] <- predictions.assay
```

We can visualize the prediction scores for some of the major expected classes.

```{r}
#| label: slideseq-prediction-plot
DefaultAssay(slide.seq) <- "predictions"

cell.types.of.interest <- c("CA3 Principal cells", "Dentate Principal cells", "Endothelial tip", "Entorhinal cortex", "Ependymal", "Oligodendrocyte")

SpatialFeaturePlot(slide.seq, features = cell.types.of.interest, alpha = c(0.1, 1))
```

```{r}
#| label: slideseq-predicted-id
slide.seq$predicted.id <- GetTransferPredictions(slide.seq)

Idents(slide.seq) <- "predicted.id"

cell.types.highlight <- CellsByIdentities(object = slide.seq, idents = cell.types.of.interest[1:3])

SpatialDimPlot(slide.seq, cells.highlight = cell.types.highlight, facet.highlight = TRUE)
```

## Identification of Spatially Variable Features

Here we demonstrate an implementation of Moran's I available via
`FindSpatiallyVariableFeatures()` by setting `method = "moransi"`. Moran's I
computes an overall spatial autocorrelation and gives a statistic (similar to a
correlation coefficient) that measures the dependence of a feature on spatial
location.

A basic binning strategy draws a rectangular grid over a Slide-seq puck and
averages the feature and location within each bin. The number of bins in the x
and y direction are controlled by the `x.cuts` and `y.cuts` parameters. While
not required, installing the optional `Rfast2` package will significantly
decrease the runtime.

::: {.callout-warning}
This is the slowest `FindSpatiallyVariableFeatures()` call in the document. The
optional `Rfast2` package speeds up the binning path. Consider precomputing.
:::

```{r}
#| label: slideseq-svf
DefaultAssay(slide.seq) <- "SCT"

slide.seq <- FindSpatiallyVariableFeatures(slide.seq,
                                           assay = "SCT",
                                           layer = "scale.data",
                                           features = VariableFeatures(slide.seq)[1:1000],
                                           selection.method = "moransi",
                                           x.cuts = 100, y.cuts = 100)
```

Now we visualize the expression of the top 6 features identified by Moran's I.

```{r}
#| label: slideseq-svf-plot
spatially.variable.feats <- SpatiallyVariableFeatures(slide.seq, method = "moransi")

SpatialFeaturePlot(slide.seq,
                   features = head(spatially.variable.feats, 6),
                   ncol = 3,
                   alpha = c(0.1, 1),
                   max.cutoff = "q95")
```

## Spatial deconvolution using RCTD

Seurat v5 also includes support for Robust Cell Type Decomposition (RCTD,
Cable et al. 2022, <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41587-021-00830-w>), a computational
approach to deconvolve spot-level data when provided with an scRNA-seq reference.

::: {.callout-important}
## RCTD requires the `spacexr` package installed from GitHub

Upstream installs it with
`devtools::install_github("dmcable/spacexr", build_vignettes = FALSE)`. That is a
network call and is **not** run here. This section assumes `spacexr` is already
installed on the machine doing the render. If it is not, the `library(spacexr)`
chunk below errors and, under `error: false`, stops the render.
:::

::: {.callout-warning}
## Do not raise `max_cores` on a workshop machine

`create.RCTD()` / `run.RCTD()` are slow, and RCTD does parallelize, but each
worker is a separate R process holding its own copy of the reference. The
hippocampus reference alone is 1.8 GB.

Measured 2026-07-26 on a 30 GB laptop: `max_cores = 8` spawned eight workers of
1.1 to 1.9 GB each, on top of a 5.5 GB parent, filling memory, pushing 39 GB
into swap, and driving load average to 34 on four physical cores. It made no
progress for over an hour and had to be killed.

`max_cores = 2` matches the worker cap in
`seurat5_spatial_vignette_2_local.qmd`, which carries the same reasoning for
`future` workers. Students have 16 GB, so treat 2 as a ceiling, not a default
to tune upward.

Precomputing the annotated object at staging time is the better answer if this
step is still too slow.
:::

```{r}
#| label: rctd-library
library(spacexr)
```

Counts, cluster, and spot information is extracted from the Seurat query and
reference objects to construct `Reference` and `SpatialRNA` objects used by RCTD.

```{r}
#| label: rctd-reference
# Set up reference
ref <- readRDS(file.path(data_dir, "mouse_hippocampus_reference.rds"))
ref <- UpdateSeuratObject(ref)
Idents(ref) <- "celltype"

# Extract information to pass to the RCTD Reference function
counts <- ref[["RNA"]]$counts
cluster <- as.factor(ref$celltype)
names(cluster) <- colnames(ref)
nUMI <- ref$nCount_RNA
names(nUMI) <- colnames(ref)
reference <- Reference(counts, cluster, nUMI)
```

```{r}
#| label: rctd-query
# Set up query with the RCTD function SpatialRNA
slide.seq <- readRDS(file.path(data_dir, "ssHippo.rds"))
counts <- slide.seq[["Spatial"]]$counts
coords <- GetTissueCoordinates(slide.seq)
colnames(coords) <- c("x", "y")
coords[is.na(colnames(coords))] <- NULL
query <- SpatialRNA(coords, counts, colSums(counts))
```

Using the reference and query object, we annotate the dataset and add the cell
type labels to the query Seurat object.

```{r}
#| label: rctd-run
# max_cores = 2, not 8. Each worker is a full copy of the reference. See the
# callout above: 8 workers filled 30 GB and 39 GB of swap without finishing.
RCTD <- create.RCTD(query, reference, max_cores = 2)
RCTD <- run.RCTD(RCTD, doublet_mode = 'doublet')
slide.seq <- AddMetaData(slide.seq, metadata = RCTD@results$results_df)
```

Next, plot the RCTD annotations. Because we ran RCTD in doublet mode, the
algorithm assigns a `first_type` and `second_type` for each barcode or spot.

```{r}
#| label: rctd-plot
p1 <- SpatialDimPlot(slide.seq, group.by = "first_type")
p2 <- SpatialDimPlot(slide.seq, group.by = "second_type")

p1 | p2
```

